  "summary": "Genome-scale and whole-cell models now quantify how resource costs, geometry and light shape optimal bacterial growth, though all results are model-based.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is constraint-based optimality: each item asks which physical or metabolic limits determine efficient bacterial growth. The coarse-grained whole-cell model ties biosynthetic efficiency to cell geometry and intracellular diffusion, reporting that efficiency peaks at overflow onset and that maximum cell size falls as growth rate rises. The enzyme-constrained GECKO model of Synechocystis PCC 6803 extends proteome allocation to light intensity, showing a proteome-limited growth decline across three conditions. Variational kinetics contributes a different scale, recasting genome-scale steady-state elementary kinetics as conic optimisation and linking metabolic flux models to kinetic descriptions. What is new is the joint treatment of resource allocation, cell geometry and kinetic feasibility within optimisation frameworks. All three are computational models.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-31"
}
